.SH NAME
git\-video \- create a video walk\-through of a change set
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
git video <rev|range> [options]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
Create a video walk\-through of a change set.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB<rev|range>\fR
a commit (shown as X^..X) or a range A..B (default HEAD).
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR\ \fI{tour,develop}\fR
tour (default) or develop.
.TP
\fB\-\-series\fR
treat a range's patches separately: one tour or one performance per commit, each on top of the one before, with a card between them (a single rev is unchanged).
.TP
\fB\-\-verbose[=1,2,3]\fR
tour: side exploration (xref visits). Unset: one visit in the whole tour; 1: up to one per file; 2: up to one per hunk; 3: every resolvable identifier (capped). develop: unset elides long typed runs and speeds up freely; 1 elides only very long runs; 2 never elides but still speeds up; 3 types everything at natural speed. A bare \-\-verbose means 1.
.TP
\fB\-\-voice[=<voice\-profile>]\fR
narrate the change, with captions: not reading the code but explaining the intent behind it, in a Kokoro voice (default bf_isabella); needs the voice extra, pip install 'git\-video[voice]', and the Kokoro model files.
.TP
\fB\-\-narrator\fR\ \fI{auto,fork\-sandbox,claude,heuristic}\fR
with \-\-voice, who writes the narration: fork\-sandbox (an agent session in a sandboxed clone reads the change and explains its approach and reasons; see github.com/mgalgs/fork\-sandbox), claude (the same brief for Claude Code, run headless and read\-only in a worktree of the change, with the login the claude command already has), heuristic (from the commit message), or auto (default: fork\-sandbox when the fork\-sandbox command is on PATH, falling back to heuristic with a warning if it fails, else heuristic). GIT_VIDEO_NARRATOR_MODEL picks the agent's model (default opus) and GIT_VIDEO_NARRATOR_ARGS adds fork\-sandbox options. A script is cached in $XDG_CACHE_HOME/git\-video/narration.
.TP
\fB\-\-duration\fR\ \fITIME\fR
target length of the whole video, cards included (60, 60s, 1m30s): the planner keeps the most important hunks that fit, in story order, and lists the files it leaves out on the closing card; it lands within a tenth of it (3s under 30s), or stays shorter, with a note, when the change is too small to fill it. Never under 15s. With \-\-verbose 3, develop still elides nothing but types at the speed the target needs.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR\ \fI{16x9,9x16,1x1,all}\fR
output shape (default 16x9).
.TP
\fB\-\-quality\fR\ \fI{high,medium,small}\fR
encoder quality: high (x264 CRF 18, the default), medium (CRF 23), small (CRF 28, about a third the size).
.TP
\fB\-o\fR\ \fIFILE\fR, \fB\-\-out\fR\ \fIFILE\fR
output MP4 (default git\-video\-<short\-sha>.mp4, or git\-video\-<a>\-<b>.mp4 for a range; with \-\-format all, \-<format> goes before the extension); a missing directory is created.
.TP
\fB\-\-jobs\fR\ \fIN\fR
render workers (default: one per CPU).
.TP
\fB\-\-plan\-json\fR
print the shot plan as JSON and exit.
.TP
\fB\-C\fR\ \fIPATH\fR
run as if started in PATH (repeatable, like git \-C).
.TP
\fB\-\-font\fR\ \fIFAMILY\fR
editor font family (default monospace).
.TP
\fB\-\-stills\fR\ \fIDIR\fR
write a PNG of each key moment plus sheet.png to DIR and exit without rendering.
.SH MODES
.TP
\fBtour\fR
A solid xref session in $EDITOR (default=Emacs). Clicks through the relevant bits of code, highlighting things along the way, clicking into relevant definitions when interesting. Keyboard clicking noises. \-\-verbose to control amount of side exploration.
.TP
\fBdevelop\fR
A pair programming session of the entire change set from top to bottom. Including rewrites when \-\-series is provided (each patch builds on the last). Programming as a performance art where the code is the main character (credit: bisqwit). Can control elission and frequency of speed\-up sequences with \-\-verbose. Default is to aim for roughly 60s per 500 lines of code.
.PP
Keyboard clicking noises, mouse clicking and highlighting when something should be highlighted. An xref visit results in a quick visual "click" on the word being drilled down into. Use side sheets and bars for things like call stack and overall orientation or supplemental information, types, classes, definitions, semantic meaning behind large blocks (which can be derived intrinsically and harvested aggressively from commit messages), etc.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBgit\fR(1), \fBffmpeg\fR(1), mgalgs/videobuilder (https://github.com/mgalgs/videobuilder)
